Let $X$ be the number of heads in 8 flips of a coin with a probability $p$ of heads coming up. Notice that we're trying to answer whether $\text{P}(X = 3) = \frac{1}{25}\text{P}(X = 5)$. To apply the binomial distribution, we need to find the ^8C_3 \, p^3\, (1-p)^5$ probability from $X =3$. Similarly, the probability for $X = 5$ is $^8C_5 \, p^5\, (1-p)^3$. We know that :

\begin{align*}
\text{P}(X = 3) = ^8C_3\, p^3\, (1-p)^5
\end{align*}

and 

\begin{align*}
\text{P}(X = 5) = ^8C_5\, p^5\, (1-p)^3
\end{align*}

Since $^8C_3 = ^8C_5$ because ${8 \choose 3} = {8\choose 5},$ we have a nice cancellation!

\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{25} &\, = \frac{\text{P}(X = 3)}{\text{P}(X = 5)} \\
&= \frac{^8C_3\, p^3\, (1-p)^5}{^8C_5\, p^5\, (1-p)^3} \\
&= \frac{^8C_3}{^8C_5} ~\cdot~ \frac{p^3\, (1-p)^5}{p^5\, (1-p)^3} \\
&= \frac{^8C_3}{^8C_5}~,~ \frac{(1-p)^2}{p^2} \\
&= 1 ~\cdot~ \frac{(1-p)^2}{p^2} \\
\frac{1}{25} &= \frac{(1-p)^2}{p^2} \\
\frac{p^2}{25} &= (1-p)^2\\
\frac{p}{5} &= 1-p\\
p &= 5\, -\, 5\, p \\
6\, p &= 5 \\
p &= \boxed{\frac{5}{6}}
{/eqn}
\end{align*}

Therefore, $p = \boxed{\frac{5}{6}}$ and $m + n = 5 + 6 = \boxed{11}$.

